<p>St. Joseph Catholic Church is significant for both its architecture and its history. Milwaukee architect {% person "anton-dohmen" %}Anton Dohmen{% endperson %} designed the church in the Romanesque style. He designed five North Dakota churches, including St. Mary's Church at Assumption Abbey in Richardton. Dohmen also submitted the original design for Bismarck's Cathedral of the Holy Spirit, although it was later rejected.</p>

<p>Mandan's first Mass was celebrated in 1879 by {% person "valentine-sommereisen" %}Father Valentine Sommereisen{% endperson %} at the Linnehan residence. The parish was organized in 1881 and secured land for a church within the city's "Catholic settlement." Construction began that fall at Stark Avenue, now Collins Avenue, and Third Street.</p>

<p>A wooden building intended to serve as both church and school was completed in October 1882, although the size of the congregation required changes to the original plan. {% person "father-cassidy", "Father Cassidy" %}Father Cassidy{% endperson %}, previously of Yankton, Dakota Territory, had been associated with the parish since its organization. By 1884, he reportedly lived in a rectory added to the original church. Friends of {% person "daniel-collins" %}Father Collins{% endperson %} in New York gave statues of the Virgin Mary and St. Joseph to the congregation in February 1897. Stations of the Cross were added several months later.</p>

<p>The congregation was initially heavily influenced by Irish members. By 1892, Germans from Ukraine had begun arriving in Morton County. Barely a decade after the first St. Joseph's Church was completed, parish records included requests for a German priest and for German to be used in services and confirmation instruction. The Irish-born priest's selection of German-born Anton Dohmen to design a larger replacement church may have been an attempt to address the desire for greater German influence in the parish.</p>

