<p>The cornerstone was laid in April 1904. A tin box beneath it contains relics from Lourdes, Rome, Bethlehem, Jerusalem, the Mount of Olives, and the Jordan River. Father Daniel Collins led construction of the current church and personally loaned $13,500 toward its $40,000 cost. After his death in 1909, he was interred in a basement vault.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/landmarks/saint-joseph-church/200-st-joseph-church-interior-homboe-kms.jpg" alt="Ornate historic interior of St. Joseph Catholic Church">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The church was dedicated in November 1904 by {% person "john-shanley" %}Bishop John Shanley{% endperson %}, North Dakota's first Catholic bishop. It seats 725, and its tower and steeple rise 114 feet. The ornate original interior used extensive stenciling to decorate and define the vaulted ceiling arches, resembling churches familiar to European immigrants.</p>

<p>The outdoor statue "Jesus Blessing" was added to the grounds in June 1921. Bishop {% person "john-wehrle" %}Wehrle{% endperson %} presided over a special Mass and dedication ceremony featuring a 300-member children's choir. The church's rosary society, St. Mary's Society, funded the statue with major donations from two local residents.</p>

<p>The church was constructed with native rock, Kasota stone facing, and Menominee brick from Wisconsin. A narthex was added in 1974 to provide a gathering space consistent with Catholic church design after the Second Vatican Council. The original stained-glass windows remain. They were most likely designed by Milwaukee artist {% person "karl-riemann" %}Karl Riemann{% endperson %} in collaboration with Dohmen. Their unusual devotional designs honor saints, and many are dedicated to pioneer priests from throughout the region.</p>
